<title>BRAZIL: Brazil's Malan mum on CMPF exemption for stocks.</title>
<headline>Brazil's Malan mum on CMPF exemption for stocks.</headline>
<dateline>BRASILIA 1996-12-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>Brazil's Finance Minister Pedro Malan said the government was studying the possibility of exempting transactions on the country's stock markets from a new cheque tax, but declined to give details.</p>
<p>&quot;I'm not going to comment on something which is under study within the government,&quot; Malan told reporters.</p>
<p>On Wednesday, Finance Ministry executive secretary Pedro Parente confirmed speculation the government would like to exempt stock market transactions from a 0.2 percent levy which would be charged from January 23 under a new tax.</p>
<p>The Provisional Contribution on Financial Transactions (CPMF) is aimed at raising funds for Brazil's health service. The 0.2 percent tax will be levied over a 12-month period.</p>
<p>-- William Schomberg, Brasilia newsroom 5561 2230358</p>
